{"agent":"skill.keyidentification","answer":"To lower the song you can transpose the progression E‑A‑B down a whole step to D‑G‑A, putting it in the key of D major. In the original key the chords are E major (E G# B), A major (A C# E), and B major (B D# F#) – a classic I‑IV‑V pattern in E major (E major is a top‑match with three diatonic chords). Because E major fits three of the four supplied chords, it’s the most likely key for the un‑transposed song. To make it easier to sing, transpose every chord down a whole step: E → D, A → G, B → A, yielding D major (D F# A), G major (G B D), and A major (A C# E). In D major the progression is I‑IV‑V, just like in E major. \r\n\r\n**Key we are working in:** D major (natural scale: D E F# G A B C#). \r\n\r\n**Roman‑numeral analysis:**\r\n- Original (E major): I (E major), IV (A major), V (B major).\r\n- Transposed (D major): I (D major), IV (G major), V (A major).\r\n\r\n**Why this works:** The data lists E major as a top match with three diatonic chords (E, A, B), meaning the progression naturally belongs to E major. By moving each chord down the same interval (a whole step), the functional relationships stay the same, just in a lower key that fits a lower vocal range. This is the standard way to transpose while preserving the song’s harmonic feel.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":3794,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":6,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":4,"id":"f-1","kind":null,"label":"skill.transpose","match":null,"prompt":"song is E A B but i cant sing that high, need it in D","routing":"orchestrator-skill-semantic","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":6}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"Yes, the chords **E** (E G♯ B) and **C** (C E G) share the note **E**.  \r\n\r\nIn the key of **C major**, the diatonic chord built on the third scale degree is **Em** (E G B), which shares **E** (and also **G**) with the **C** major chord (C E G). If you’re using the **E major** chord (E G♯ B) instead of E minor, the only common tone with **C major** remains **E**.  \r\n\r\nSo:\r\n\r\n- **C** major = (C E G)  \r\n- **E** major = (E G♯ B) – common tone **E**  \r\n- **E** minor = (E G B) – common tones **E** and **G**  \r\n\r\nWorking in **C major** gives you the natural (diatonic) pair **C** major and **E minor**, sharing two notes, while **E major** can still be used as a chromatic borrowing, sharing the single note **E**.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":2395,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":6,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":3,"id":"f-12","kind":null,"label":"skill.commontones","match":null,"prompt":"do E and C have any notes in common at all","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":5}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"The distance from G up to E is a **major sixth**.  \r\n\r\nA major sixth spans nine semitones (G → G♯ → A → A♯ → B → C → C♯ → D → E). In diatonic harmony it appears as the interval between the tonic and the submediant in a major key (e.g., G is the tonic, E is the sixth degree of G major). This interval gives a warm, expansive sound and is often used in chord extensions and melody lines.\r\n\r\n**Example chords that contain a major sixth interval:**\r\n\r\n- **G6** = G – B – D – E (`(G B D E)`)\r\n- **Cmaj13** = C – E – G – B – D – F – A (`(C E G B D F A)`) – the G–E pair inside is a major sixth.\r\n- **Am(add6)** = A – C – E – F♯ (`(A C E F#)`) – the E–C pair is a minor sixth, but the chord shows how adding the 6th creates that interval.\r\n\r\n**Key context:** In the key of **G major**, the notes G and E form the tonic (I) and the submediant (vi) scale degrees, respectively, making the interval a diatonic major sixth.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":2183,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":1,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":1,"id":"f-20","kind":null,"label":"skill.interval","match":null,"prompt":"from G up to E what do you call that distance","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":["G6 = G - B - D - E"],"verdict":"T","verified":1}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"In D major the diatonic chords are D (D‑F♯‑A), Em (E‑G‑B), F♯m (F♯‑A‑C♯), G (G‑B‑D), A (A‑C♯‑E), Bm (B‑D‑F♯), and C♯dim (C♯‑E‑G).  \r\n\r\nThese seven chords are built on each scale degree of the D major scale (D E F♯ G A B C♯) and naturally fit any progression in this key. The I chord (D) is the tonal center, the ii (Em) and vi (Bm) provide smooth step‑wise motion, the iii (F♯m) and IV (G) add color, the V (A) creates a strong dominant‑to‑tonic resolution, and the vii° (C♯dim) functions as a leading‑tone chord that resolves to the tonic.  \r\n\r\nYou can also enrich the palette with common extensions and inversions, such as Dmaj7 (D‑F♯‑A‑C♯), G6 (G‑B‑D‑E), A7 (A‑C♯‑E‑G), or secondary dominants like E7 (E‑G♯‑B‑D) leading to A, all of which remain diatonically compatible when used judiciously in D major.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":2554,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":14,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":2,"id":"f-28","kind":null,"label":"skill.diatonicchords","match":null,"prompt":"chords that work in D major?","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":["Dmaj7 (D-F#-A-C#)","G6 (G-B-D-E)","A7 (A-C#-E-G)","E7 (E-G#-B-D)"],"verdict":"F","verified":7}
