{"agent":"skill.keyidentification","answer":"The transposed chords up a whole step are Bm (B D F#), G (G B D), D (D F# A), and A (A C# E).  In the original progression Am – F – C – G the strongest key candidates were C major (relative A minor) because all four chords are diatonic to those keys.  Raising everything by a whole step moves the tonal center up two semitones, so the new progression lands in D major (the parallel major of the original C major) where the same chord qualities appear as vi – IV – I – V: Bm (vi), G (IV), D (I), A (V).  D major scale: D E F# G A B C#.  In guitar‑friendly terms, play Bm (2‑5‑7 on the neck), G (3‑2‑0), D (open D shape) and A (open A shape) to get the same feel but a whole step higher.  If you heard the music resolve strongly to D, that confirms D major; if it felt darker and resolved to B, it could be B minor, but the major‑key function vi‑IV‑I‑V is most common in pop/rock, so D major is the most likely.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":4005,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":5,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"f-73","kind":null,"label":"skill.transpose","match":null,"prompt":"put Am F C G up a whole step pls","routing":"orchestrator-skill-semantic","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":4}
